<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Island Main Trunk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as North Island Main Trunk railway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The North Island Main Trunk is the railway line linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, completed in 1908. Its central section ran through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-country">King Country</a>, which was closed to Europeans until Māori leaders allowed surveys in the 1880s. Its engineering works include the Raurimu Spiral and several high viaducts, and the line opened up the interior of the North Island to settlement and farming.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-island-main-trunk.jpg" alt="North Island Main Trunk" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joerg Mueller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_North_Island_Main_Trunk_Line,_August_2024.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
